        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-08T07:58:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "IIRC the only other case when we are using such temporary share/unshare is for shaped_apic in `setup_vcpu_lapic()` (and I\u0027m not sure if copying would be cleaner than sharing in that case). So I\u0027m not sure if it\u0027s worth introducing another API for such a corner case.\n\nAs for those IOMMU hypercalls, I\u0027m not sure taking host_mmu_lock etc in each such hypercall every time would be better than simply passing all params in-band in registers.\n\nBTW, IIRC, the only reason we need up to 10 registers for that is that we pass a 4-byte pkvm_memcache struct in the domain map hypercall, every time. And I doubt we actually _need_ to do it this way to provide the memcache. For MMU we don\u0027t do it this way.",
      "parentUuid": "f6f4ee1a_13853bc4",
      "revId": "4930933f964dfd7608a24a6b483fe396c2619203",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "13fa862f_24e534bf",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/gsmi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 84,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-08T09:31:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Fixed the TOCTOU, using READ_ONCE.\n\nBTW regarding this:\n\n\u003e To be 100% safe in this regard, we should probably copy it via *bounce_sel \u003d *(volatile struct gsmi_set_eventlog_param *)sel instead, like we are doing e.g. in pkvm_refill_memcache().\n\nI\u0027ve done a bit of research on this with gemini\u0027s help, and it looks like generally we\u0027d better not do that (i.e. not rely on volatile semantics for structs or other non-scalar types), at least because that seems to be poorly supported by compilers, e.g.: https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id\u003d111849\n\nSo for this patch I\u0027ve just used READ_ONCE for each individual field instead. For the few existing cases like pkvm_refill_memcache(), we can fix them later.",
      "parentUuid": "b7d73341_6ad37627",
      "revId": "4930933f964dfd7608a24a6b483fe396c2619203",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9e89261d_53f3d5e0",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/gsmi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 84,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-08T10:58:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e So for this patch I\u0027ve just used READ_ONCE for each individual field instead.\n\nI was thinking if memcpy would be sufficient here. Hypercall boundary will act as a barrier and there would not be any optimization based re-orderings on this cpu. A well-behaved host will not be touching this from another cpu. A buggy/malicious host modifying the contents of sel from another cpu cannot pose a security threat in this case I guess - worst case is a bad data_ptr, data_len and/or type and they all are checked and validated before we proceed.\n\nBut I agree READ_ONCE is a formal hygiene in the generic sense and doesn\u0027t cost anything extra.",
      "parentUuid": "13fa862f_24e534bf",
      "revId": "4930933f964dfd7608a24a6b483fe396c2619203",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
